    <statement><p>Find a parameterization <m>(x(t),y(t),z(t))</m> for the
curve
<m>y = 4+4x^{4}</m> that is parallel to
the <m>xy</m>-plane.  Your parametrized curve should pass through the point
<m>(0, 4 , -3)</m> when <m>t = -2</m>, and have <m>x'(-2)\neq 0</m>.</p>
<p><m>x(t) =</m> <fillin characters="35" name="AnSwEr0001"/>,</p>
<p><m>y(t) =</m> <fillin characters="35" name="AnSwEr0002"/>,</p>
<p><m>z(t) =</m> <fillin characters="35" name="AnSwEr0003"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>t--2</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>4+4\mathopen{}\left(t--2\right)^{4}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>-3</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><m>z</m> is constant since the curve is parallel to the <m>xy</m>-plane, so <m>z=-3</m> 
since it passes through <m>(0,4,-3)</m>. One possible answer
is
<me>x = t+2, \quad y = 4+4\mathopen{}\left(t+2\right)^{4}, \quad z=-3,</me>
which passes through the indicated point, with <m>x'(-2)=1</m>.



</solution>
